{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-20574",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's illustrate the triangles.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14233096/assets/2e18009a3a454e83e42f9f21840c6821605ac2eb8f4e3d493c8468a5fe5f5acc.png)\n\n \n\nWe're told that the perimeter of the equilateral triangle $△ABC$ is ${p}_{1}=9,$ and since all $3$ sides have equal length, we get\n\n$$\nAB=\\frac{{p}_{1}}{3}=\\frac{9}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSince  $△ABD$  is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we have $AD=BD=5.$ \n\nNow, we have enough information to compute the perimeter of the triangle $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BD+AD \\\\  & =3+5+5 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
